@using CivicLens.Application.Publication
@using CivicLens.Host.Components.Layout
@using CivicLens.Publication.Contracts

<PublicPage Title="How the talk and action profile works" Description="How Civic Lens builds the talk and action profile of each official. Code builds the profile from published records. No model is used." Crumbs="Crumbs">
    <PageHeader Title="How the talk and action profile works" Lede="Code builds the profile on each official page. No model is used." />
    <Panel Title="What the issue counts mean" Padded="true">
        <p>The panel "Issues they work on" has one row for each tracked issue. Each row has three counts.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Their statements: the published statements of the official on the issue. A statement counts in the Congress of its day. The day is the statement day, or the first publication day when the statement has no day.</li>
            <li>Bills they sponsored: the published bills on the issue that name the official as sponsor. The row lists the official title of each bill. Cosponsors do not count. A bill counts in the Congress of its latest sponsorship day.</li>
            <li>Votes they cast: the substantive roll call votes on the issue where the official cast a vote. A roll call counts once. A cast of Not Voting does not count. Any other cast counts.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each count is a number of records. It is not a share, a rank, or a measure of how much the official cares about the issue. A record on several issues counts once for each issue. A record with no tracked issue is in no row.</p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel Title="Missed votes" Padded="true">
        <p>@PublishedOfficialView.MissedVoteMeaning The count covers all roll calls of the current Congress, in each chamber where the official has votes. It includes procedural roll calls.</p>
        <p>The total is the number of published roll calls that the chamber held while the official served. A roll call that was not published is in neither number.</p>
        <p>The page shows a share only when the total is at least @TalkAndActionProfile.MinimumRecordsForShare roll calls.</p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel Title="Votes with their party" Padded="true">
        <p>@PublishedOfficialView.SplitVoteMeaning The majority of a party is the Yea or Nay cast that more of its members made. A party with equal Yea and Nay counts has no majority, so its roll call is not a split vote. A vote for a candidate, such as the election of the Speaker, is never a split vote.</p>
        <p>The page counts only split votes where the official cast Yea or Nay. Aye counts as Yea and No counts as Nay. The official votes with the party when the cast equals the majority of the official party in the same chamber. The party is the party of the official on the day of the vote.</p>
        <p>A cast of Present or Not Voting is not in either number. The page states how many split votes it left out. It shows a percent only when the official cast Yea or Nay on at least @TalkAndActionProfile.MinimumRecordsForShare split votes.</p>
        <p>The page leaves out votes that the official cast while in a party other than @PublishedPartyMajority.Democrat or @PublishedPartyMajority.Republican.</p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel Title="What a link is" Padded="true">
        <p>A link joins one statement to one bill or one vote. A reviewer approved each link. The reviewer read the statement and the bill or vote, and judged that the statement is about it.</p>
        <p>There are four kinds of link. "Describes own bill" means the statement describes a bill that the speaker sponsors or cosponsors. "States position on" means the statement says what the speaker thinks or wants about a bill. "Claims credit for" means the statement says the speaker caused or achieved a bill, or part of it. "Statement about a vote" means the statement is about a roll call vote.</p>
        <p>A link is not a verdict. It does not say that the vote or the bill matches the statement. The page shows the vote of the official next to the link and leaves the judgment to the reader. A link belongs to the Congress of the day of its statement. A statement with no day belongs to the Congress of its bill or vote.</p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel Title="The window" Padded="true">
        <p>All three counts use the current Congress. The current Congress of an official is the newest Congress with a published vote of that official.</p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel Title="Substantive votes" Padded="true">
        <p>A vote is substantive when its kind is one of these: @SubstantiveKinds. The count of votes they cast leaves out all other kinds, such as rules, cloture, and motions. These are procedural votes.</p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel Title="Where the issues come from" Padded="true">
        <p>A person reviews the issue tags of a statement and approves them. Some tags can still be wrong. The issues of a sponsorship and of a vote come from the policy area of the bill in Congress.gov.</p>
    </Panel>
</PublicPage>

@code {
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<Crumb> Crumbs = [PublicCrumbs.Home(""), new("How the talk and action profile works")];

    private static string SubstantiveKinds => string.Join(", ", Enum.GetValues<PublishedVoteKind>()
        .Where(kind => kind.IsSubstantive()).Select(PublishedRecordLabels.VoteKind));
}
